{"diagnosis":"Race condition in read-write lock usage detected and repaired.","root_cause":"The read-write lock is held during a check-then-act sequence that modifies shared state, but the lock is not held across the entire critical section, leading to a race condition where multiple threads can observe the same prior value and increment concurrently, resulting in lost updates.","confidence":0.95,"reasoning_summary":"The update function holds the read lock only during a check-then-act loop that modifies shared state. This violates the invariant that the lock must be held for the entire critical section. Without the lock held during the write, multiple threads can read the same prior value, leading to lost increments. The fix ensures the lock is held throughout the entire update loop.","expected_effect":"All updates are correctly serialized, ensuring the final value is exactly 4000 after two threads each perform 2000 increments.","risk_notes":["The fix assumes the read-write lock is the correct synchronization primitive; if writer contention is high, performance may degrade.","The fix does not address potential starvation under high contention.","The fix relies on the lock being held for the entire critical section; any future modification must preserve this invariant."],"edits":[{"file":"solution.c","search":"void update(void) {
    pthread_rwlock_rdlock(&guard);
    for (int i = 0; i < 2000; ++i) {
        int prior = value;
        sched_yield();
        value = prior + 1;
    }
    pthread_rwlock_unlock(&guard);
}","replacement":"void update(void) {
    pthread_rwlock_wrlock(&guard);
    for (int i = 0; i < 2000; ++i) {
        int prior = value;
        sched_yield();
        value = prior + 1;
    }
    pthread_rwlock_unlock(&guard);
}"}]}
